course: dictionary
lesson: 6686
title: Obstacles in the way
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: An obstacle is something in your way that stops you or makes it hard to go forward. It can be a real thing you could bump into, or a problem that makes a task difficult. «Stop sledding when it gets dark, because you cannot see obstacles.» Here an obstacle is something on the hill you could crash into.
explain: Context helps again. «Sledding is done feet first, sitting up, on a hill away from roads, trees and ponds; head first, your head hits any obstacle first.» The clues are trees, ponds and head hits: an obstacle is something hard that you could crash into.
explain: An obstacle can also be a difficulty, not a thing. An obstacle course on sports day has tunnels and hurdles, but in a story the hero might overcome an obstacle, a problem stopping them from reaching their goal. Ask yourself: is the thing in the way solid, or is it a problem? Either way, it is an obstacle.
fact: An obstacle is something in your way, or a problem that makes something hard.
fact: «Obstacles» is the plural: one obstacle, many obstacles.
fact: Bats are very good at avoiding obstacles, so they do not get tangled in people's hair.
fact: Jumping spiders plan a route round obstacles to reach their prey.
fact: An obstacle course is a race with tunnels, hurdles and nets that you climb, crawl or jump through.
exercise: «Stop sledding when it gets dark, because you cannot see obstacles.» What is an obstacle here? | A warm, sunny hill | *Something in your way that you could bump into | A sled made of wood | A kind friend who helps you
exercise: Which of these is an obstacle on the pavement for someone walking? | Clean, empty pavement | A friendly dog wagging its tail | *A bin left in the way | A bright red bus
exercise: Mia says her maths test was an obstacle. What does she mean? | *The test was a difficulty that made it hard for her to do well | The test was over very quickly | She lost her pencil case | The test was a lovely day out
test: What is the plural of obstacle? | obstacle | *obstacles | obstacless | obstacle's
test: «He overcame the obstacle and finished the race.» What does overcome mean here? | Ran away from it | Gave up on it | Made it bigger | *Got past it
exam: «Bats are very good at avoiding obstacles.» What does avoiding mean? | Counting | *Staying away from | Painting | Crashing into
